I see the full HelpCommand class. The ExecuteAsync method calls Logger.LogInformation in three places, including the one on line 53 you mentioned. To test this, I will need to mock ILogger<HelpCommand> and verify that LogInformation is called with the expected string.

I also see that ExecuteAsync depends on IOptions<AbpCliOptions> and IServiceScopeFactory. The IServiceScopeFactory is used to create a scope, from which a command instance is resolved and its GetUsageInfo method is called.

I will write tests for these scenarios:
- When commandLineArgs.Target is null or whitespace, LogInformation is called with GetUsageInfo result.
- When commandLineArgs.Target is not found in AbpCliOptions.Commands, LogWarning and LogInformation are called.
- When commandLineArgs.Target is found, the command is resolved from the scope and LogInformation is called with command.GetUsageInfo().

I will mock the dependencies and ILogger to verify the calls.

I will submit the test file now.

